{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786209992,"nonce":"OkCyh8HTTISsmvYveSfP+7eDZQc=","title":"**What is wrong.** `refire_grace` (10m) and the flap thresholds (5-in-30m) are","message":"guesses. `docs/ORCHESTRATION.md:144-146` lists them under \"Questions genuinely\nneeding the owner\" — \"the knobs that decide how noisy Slack is. Should be\nvalidated against a real `alertmanager.yml` before they harden.\" They are now\nper-org settings with server-enforced bounds and stated failure modes\n(`internal/identity/domain/settings.go:114-141`, commit `a827f4a`), and\n`docs/setup/tuning.md` derives them arithmetically from `group_wait`,\n`group_interval` and `repeat_interval` — but the derivation has never been run\nagainst a real cluster's numbers, and the tuning UI defaults to Alertmanager's\nown defaults with an \"Assumed, not yours\" banner.\n\n**Why it matters.** These knobs are the whole difference between oto being useful\nand oto being the thing that made Slack unreadable. `refire_grace` too low means a\nnew thread per transition; too high means a genuine re-fire is silently folded\ninto a stale thread and nobody is told. The defaults are what almost every\ninstallation will run, so a wrong default is the product's actual behaviour.\n\n**Where.** `internal/identity/domain/settings.go`, `docs/setup/tuning.md`,\n`web/src/features/settings/TuningSection.tsx`, `docs/ORCHESTRATION.md:144-146`.\n\n**Done when.** The defaults have been checked against at least one real\nproduction-shaped `alertmanager.yml` and a real set of rule `for:` durations,\neach default is justified in `docs/setup/tuning.md` by reference to those numbers\nrather than to a plausible guess, and any default that does not survive contact is\nchanged. Blocked on the owner or on a willing operator, not on code.","files":null}]}